.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-restore-unit 1  "tapectl-restore-unit " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-restore\-unit \- Restore a unit from a volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl restore unit\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-unit\fR] [\fB\-\-from\fR] <\fB\-\-to\fR> [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-scratch\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-overwrite\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-space\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-spool\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIUNIT\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Restore a unit from a volume
.PP
Exit status: 0 = restored; 2 = an error or a refusal; 75 = the catalog was busy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-unit\fR \fI<UNIT>\fR
The unit, as a flag: the same as UNIT
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fI<FROM>\fR
Volume label.

Without it the loaded tape names itself: File 0 is read and the volume is found in this catalog by its uuid, and the one chosen is printed. A label that is given is checked against File 0 as ever. A tape this catalog does not know is refused, naming what File 0 says. Without it, \-\-dry\-run (which opens no drive) previews from the one volume this catalog has holding the unit, and names it; when several hold it they are listed, to name one here. For a version held across cartridges (a range on this tape), this tape (or the loaded one) must hold its slice 1: the others are asked for in slice order, by label, sticker and chip serial, and a tape that does not hold slice 1 is refused, naming the ones that do
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fI<TO>\fR
Destination directory
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path).

Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR \fI<VERSION>\fR
Snapshot version to restore.

Defaults to the newest version of the unit on this volume (the same rule RESTORE.sh applies); a version the volume does not carry is refused, naming the ones it does
.TP
\fB\-\-scratch\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Where the restore\*(Aqs scratch directory is made (default: inside \-\-to).

Where the restore\*(Aqs scratch directory is made (removed when the restore ends); defaults to inside \-\-to, never the system temp directory. A unit whose isolated catalogue from `stage create` is on disk streams its slices into dar and puts only named pipes there; otherwise (a rebuilt catalog, dar older than 2.7.9) every decrypted slice waits there, as large as the unit
.TP
\fB\-\-overwrite\fR
Restore into a destination that already holds files, replacing any that collide.

Without it a destination that is not empty is refused before the tape is touched
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-space\-check\fR
Skip the free\-space check, for a filesystem that holds more than it reports.

Skip the free\-space check (the unit\*(Aqs size when it streams; about twice that when its slices are spooled, with the scratch directory and \-\-to on one disk), for a filesystem that holds more than it reports free, such as a compressed or thin\-provisioned one
.TP
\fB\-\-spool\fR
Decrypt every slice to the scratch directory before dar extracts them.

Decrypt every slice to the scratch directory before dar extracts them, even when the unit could stream (its isolated catalogue on disk). Needs about twice the unit\*(Aqs size with the scratch directory and \-\-to on one disk. Across cartridges too: a version held across cartridges is read from each in turn into the scratch directory, and dar extracts once the last is read; without it the slices stream into one extraction across the swaps
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
An age identity file (a secret key) to decrypt with, in this command only.

An age identity file (the SECRET, `AGE\-SECRET\-KEY\-1…`) to decrypt with in this command only; repeat for several. It is added to the home\*(Aqs own keys and nothing more: never copied into the home, registered as any key, or logged. Lets an heir who holds only the escrow key restore through tapectl after `catalog rebuild \-\-from\-volume \-\-key`. A file that is not an identity is refused, naming it, before the drive is opened
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be restored without restoring
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fIUNIT\fR]
Unit name
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
  Restore the newest version of a unit from the loaded tape:
    tapectl restore unit photos \-\-to /srv/restore/photos
  An older version, from a named volume:
    tapectl restore unit photos \-\-from L6\-0001 \-\-version 2 \-\-to /srv/restore/photos
  An heir with only the escrow key, after catalog rebuild \-\-from\-volume \-\-key:
    tapectl restore unit \-\-unit photos \-\-device /dev/tape/by\-id/YOUR\-DRIVE\-nst \-\-to /srv/restore/photos \-\-key escrow.key
.fi
